window.SITE = {
 title: "Classical Myth in Art",
 sections: {1:"Myth and Its World", 2:"The Beginning of the World", 3:"The Olympians", 4:"Fertility and Death", 5:"Heroes of Argos", 6:"Heroes of Athens and Thebes", 7:"Troy and the Journey Home", 8:"Rome and the Meaning of Myth"},
 handouts: [
 /* ================= WEEK 1 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:1, wk:1, title:"The Nature of Myth", sub:"What myth is, the kinds of myth, and the Olympian gods",
  overview:{img:"h1_council", cap:["Giovanni Jacopo Caraglio after Raphael","Assembly of the Gods","engraving, 16th century"],
   sum:"A myth is a traditional story that a whole community treats as important. It has a plot, characters in conflict, and a setting in the distant past, and it was passed along by word of mouth long before anyone wrote it down. Because every teller changed the details, Greek myths never had one official version. This section introduces the main kinds of myth and the twelve Olympian gods who drive most of the stories that later artists painted and carved.",
   bul:["Myth = story with a plot, conflict, and a far-off past","Three kinds: divine myth, legend, folktale","Greek gods kept by Rome under Latin names"],
   terms:[["Myth","A traditional story with importance for a whole community, not just one teller; the subject of every work on this site."],["Mythos","The Greek word behind “myth,” first meaning an authoritative spoken account rather than a made-up tale."],["Pantheon","The full set of a culture’s gods taken as a group, such as the Greek Olympians."],["Divine myth","A story whose main actors are gods and other supernatural beings, set before human time (for example, the war of gods and Giants)."],["Legend (saga)","A story about human heroes, often nobles, set in real places such as Troy or Athens; it works like ancient history."],["Folktale","A story about ordinary people or talking animals told to entertain and teach social rules, like Aesop’s fables."],["Oral tradition","Passing stories along by memory and performance; it explains why the same myth survives in different versions."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"theme", title:"Myths explain the world", img:"h1_hades", stmt:"Many myths were the ancient answer to questions that science answers today.",
   para:"Why do volcanoes rumble? Because Zeus pinned the monster Typhon under Mount Etna. Why does winter come? Because Hades carried Persephone to the underworld and her mother Demeter grieves while she is gone. Stories like these, which explain how something came to be, are called etiological myths, and they run through almost every story in this guide.",
   bul:["Etiology = explanation of a cause or origin","Seasons: Persephone’s stay with Hades","Volcanoes: Typhon trapped under Etna"],
   terms:[["Etiological tale","A myth that explains the origin of a natural event, custom, or ritual; many divine myths do this."]],
   works:["h1_hadesB","h1_persephone","h3_typhon"]},
  {type:"theme", title:"No single true version", img:"h1_hermes", stmt:"Every poet, playwright, and vase painter retold a myth his own way.",
   para:"Greek myths circulated orally for centuries before Homer and Hesiod wrote versions down around 700 BCE. Later poets, playwrights, sculptors, and painters changed names, motives, and endings to suit their audiences. There was no sacred book and no church to decide which version was correct, so comparing versions is part of studying myth, and images are often our only source for a version no text preserves.",
   bul:["Written down first in the Archaic period (700s BCE)","No authoritative text or priesthood","Vase paintings preserve their own versions"],
   terms:[], works:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_zeus", artist:"", title:"Jupiter of Smyrna", date:"Roman, 2nd century CE", det:"Marble · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A Roman statue of the chief god, found at Smyrna (modern İzmir) in 1680 and sent to Louis XIV; the arm with the thunderbolt and the eagle are later restorations.",
   sum:"Zeus, called Jupiter by the Romans, is the king of the gods and the god of the sky and thunder. He is the youngest son of the Titans Cronus and Rhea and became ruler after overthrowing his father. As judge of oaths and guest-friendship he stands for order and justice, yet the myths also give him more lovers than any other god, which explains the many demigod heroes. The sculptor shows him the standard way: a mature bearded man, bare-chested, with a scepter and thunderbolt.",
   bul:["King of the gods; sky, weather, thunder","Attributes: thunderbolt, eagle, scepter, oak","Brother-husband of Hera"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_hera", artist:"", title:"Campana Hera", date:"Roman, 2nd century CE, after a Greek model", det:"Marble · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A Roman statue of the goddess, named for the Campana collection it came from; statues of this kind stood in temples, public buildings, and wealthy homes.",
   sum:"Hera, the Roman Juno, is queen of the gods and goddess of marriage and the family. She is Zeus’s sister and wife, and in myth she punishes his lovers and their children, most famously Heracles, whose name means “glory of Hera.” The statue shows her fully dressed and dignified, the opposite of the nude goddesses of love. Her peacock, a common attribute, carries the hundred eyes of her watchman Argus.",
   bul:["Queen of the gods; marriage and childbirth","Attributes: diadem, scepter, peacock, pomegranate","Pursues Zeus’s lovers and children"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_poseidon", artist:"", title:"Bronze god from Cape Artemision", date:"Greek, ca. 460 BCE", det:"Bronze · National Archaeological Museum, Athens",
   fn:"A large sanctuary statue lost in an ancient shipwreck and recovered from the sea in 1926–28; it may have been made as an offering to the god.",
   sum:"Poseidon, the Roman Neptune, rules the sea, earthquakes, and horses, and he is a brother of Zeus and Hades. This bronze once held a weapon that is now lost, so scholars still debate whether it showed Poseidon with his trident or Zeus with a thunderbolt. Either way it shows the Early Classical ideal: a perfectly balanced, mature body caught at the moment before a throw. Bronzes this size rarely survive, because most were melted down for their metal.",
   bul:["God of sea, earthquakes, horses","Attributes: trident, dolphin, horse, bull","Identity debated: Poseidon or Zeus"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_demeter", artist:"", title:"Great Eleusinian Relief", date:"Greek, ca. 440–430 BCE", det:"Marble · National Archaeological Museum, Athens",
   fn:"A votive relief from the sanctuary of Demeter at Eleusis, the site of the secret rites known as the Eleusinian Mysteries.",
   sum:"Demeter, the Roman Ceres, is the goddess of grain, farming, and the seasons; the word “cereal” comes from her Latin name. Here she hands ears of grain to the young prince Triptolemus, who will teach farming to humankind, while her daughter Persephone, holding a torch, crowns him. The relief stood at Eleusis, where worshippers were initiated into rites believed to give hope for a better afterlife.",
   bul:["Goddess of grain and harvest","Attributes: sheaf of wheat, torch, poppy","Mother of Persephone; center of Eleusinian Mysteries"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_persephone", artist:"Frederic Leighton", title:"The Return of Persephone", date:"1891", det:"Oil on canvas · Leeds Art Gallery",
   fn:"An exhibition painting by the president of the Royal Academy, made for a Victorian audience that loved classical subjects.",
   sum:"Persephone, the Roman Proserpina, is Demeter’s daughter and became queen of the underworld after Hades carried her off. Because she had eaten pomegranate seeds there, she must return to him for part of each year, and her absence explains winter. Leighton paints the moment Hermes lifts her out of the dark toward her mother, who reaches up in the sunlight. The story turns a family’s grief into the cycle of the seasons.",
   bul:["Queen of the underworld; goddess of spring","Pomegranate seeds bind her to Hades","Her yearly return explains the seasons"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_athena", artist:"", title:"Mattei Athena", date:"Roman copy of a Greek bronze of the mid-4th century BCE", det:"Marble · Musée du Louvre; on view at Louvre Abu Dhabi",
   fn:"A Roman copy of a Greek cult-type statue; wealthy Romans collected copies of famous Greek sculpture for villas and public buildings.",
   sum:"Athena, the Roman Minerva, is the virgin goddess of wisdom, crafts such as weaving, and the planning side of war. She was born fully armed from Zeus’s head after he swallowed her mother, the Titaness Metis (“cleverness”). She wears a helmet and the aegis, a goatskin cape bearing the Gorgon’s head. Athens, named for her, saw her as its protector.",
   bul:["Wisdom, crafts, strategic war","Attributes: helmet, spear, aegis with Gorgon head, owl, olive","Patron goddess of Athens"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_hestia", artist:"", title:"Hestia Giustiniani", date:"Roman copy after a Greek original of ca. 470–460 BCE", det:"Marble · Torlonia Collection, Rome · photographed on loan to the Louvre, 2024",
   fn:"A Roman copy of an Early Classical statue of a standing goddess; the identification as Hestia is traditional rather than certain.",
   sum:"Hestia, the Roman Vesta, is the goddess of the hearth, the fire at the center of every home and city. She has almost no stories of her own, because her role was simply to be present wherever a fire was kept. In Rome the Vestal Virgins tended the city’s sacred flame. Some later lists of the Twelve Olympians drop Hestia and include Dionysus instead.",
   bul:["Goddess of the hearth and household order","Sacred flame; Vestal Virgins in Rome","Sometimes replaced by Dionysus among the Twelve"], terms:[["Twelve Olympians","The main gods who live on Mount Olympus; the lists vary slightly, and Hades, who lives below, is usually left out."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_dionysus", artist:"Titian", title:"Bacchus and Ariadne", date:"1520–23", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery, London",
   fn:"Painted for the private study of Alfonso d’Este, Duke of Ferrara, as part of a series of mythological scenes.",
   sum:"Dionysus, the Roman Bacchus, is the god of wine, ecstasy, and theater, and the only Olympian with a mortal mother, the Theban princess Semele. Titian shows him leaping from his leopard-drawn chariot toward Ariadne, the Cretan princess abandoned by Theseus, whom he marries and later places in the sky as a constellation. His noisy followers, the satyrs and maenads, fill the right side.",
   bul:["Wine, revelry, theater","Attributes: grapevine, ivy, wine cup, leopard","Mother Semele was mortal"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_apollo", artist:"", title:"Apollo Belvedere", date:"Roman copy, ca. 120–140 CE, after a Greek bronze of ca. 330 BCE", det:"Marble · Vatican Museums",
   fn:"A Roman copy of a famous Greek statue; rediscovered in the late 1400s, it became a model of ideal male beauty for Renaissance and later artists.",
   sum:"Apollo, whose name stayed the same in Latin, is the god of light, music, poetry, prophecy, healing, and archery. He is the son of Zeus and Leto and the twin of Artemis. His oracle at Delphi was the most important in Greece. The statue shows him having just shot an arrow, his bow once in the outstretched left hand.",
   bul:["Light, music, prophecy, archery","Attributes: bow, lyre, laurel","Oracle at Delphi; twin of Artemis"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_artemis", artist:"", title:"Diana of Versailles", date:"Roman copy, 1st–2nd century CE, of a Greek bronze of ca. 325 BCE", det:"Marble · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A Roman copy that was given to the French king Henri II in 1556 and displayed at Versailles.",
   sum:"Artemis, the Roman Diana, is the virgin goddess of the hunt, wild animals, the moon, and childbirth. Twin sister of Apollo, she lives in the wilderness among nymphs. Here she reaches for an arrow from her quiver while a deer runs beside her. Ancient art keeps her clothed; stories warn of what happens to men who see her bathing, like the hunter Actaeon.",
   bul:["Hunt, wilderness, moon, childbirth","Attributes: bow and quiver, deer, hunting dog","Protector of girls before marriage"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_ares", artist:"", title:"Ludovisi Ares", date:"Roman copy of a Greek original of ca. 320 BCE", det:"Marble, restored by Bernini in 1622 · Palazzo Altemps, Rome",
   fn:"A Roman decorative statue later restored and displayed in the Ludovisi family collection.",
   sum:"Ares, the Roman Mars, is the god of war in its violent, bloody form, unlike Athena, who stands for planning and strategy. The Greeks disliked him, but the Romans honored Mars as the father of Romulus, and the month of March is named for him. Here he sits at rest with his shield, and a small Eros at his feet hints at his love affair with Aphrodite.",
   bul:["Violent, physical side of war","Attributes: helmet, spear, shield","Lover of Aphrodite; honored by Rome as Mars"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_aphrodite", artist:"Sandro Botticelli", title:"The Birth of Venus", date:"ca. 1484–86", det:"Tempera on canvas · Uffizi Gallery, Florence",
   fn:"Probably made for a member of the Medici circle in Florence, as a large painting for a private residence.",
   sum:"Aphrodite, the Roman Venus, is the goddess of love, beauty, and desire. In Hesiod she rises from sea foam after Cronus throws his father Uranus’s severed genitals into the sea, while Homer calls her a daughter of Zeus and Dione. Botticelli shows her arriving on a shell, blown ashore by the winds and met by a goddess of the seasons. She is married to Hephaestus but loves Ares.",
   bul:["Love, beauty, desire","Attributes: dove, rose, myrtle, shell","Born from sea foam (Hesiod)"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_hephaestus", artist:"Diego Velázquez", title:"Apollo in the Forge of Vulcan", date:"1630", det:"Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid",
   fn:"Painted during Velázquez’s first stay in Italy and later acquired for the Spanish royal collection.",
   sum:"Hephaestus, the Roman Vulcan, is the smith and craftsman of the gods, the maker of their weapons and palaces, and the only Olympian with a physical disability. Velázquez shows Apollo arriving in his forge to tell him that his wife, Aphrodite, is having an affair with Ares, and the workers freeze in shock. The word “volcano” comes from Vulcan, whose forge was imagined beneath the earth.",
   bul:["Fire, forge, metalwork","Attributes: hammer, anvil, tongs","Husband of Aphrodite"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_hermes", artist:"Attributed to Praxiteles", title:"Hermes and the Infant Dionysus", date:"ca. 340–330 BCE (some scholars date it later)", det:"Marble · Archaeological Museum of Olympia · shown in a 19th-century photograph",
   fn:"A statue dedicated in the Temple of Hera at Olympia, where the traveler Pausanias saw it in the 2nd century CE; excavators found it there in 1877.",
   sum:"Hermes, the Roman Mercury, is the messenger of the gods, the god of travelers, trade, and thieves, and the guide who leads souls to the underworld. He is the son of Zeus and the nymph Maia. Here he carries his baby half-brother Dionysus to the nymphs who will raise him, safe from Hera’s anger, and once dangled a bunch of grapes in his missing right hand. The relaxed S-curve pose and soft, polished surfaces made Praxiteles the most admired sculptor of the 4th century BCE.",
   bul:["Messenger; travelers, trade, thieves","Attributes: caduceus, petasos (traveler’s hat), winged sandals","Leads souls to Hades; protects the infant Dionysus"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h1_hadesB", artist:"", title:"Pluto (as Serapis) and Persephone (as Isis) with Cerberus", date:"Roman, 2nd century CE", det:"Marble · Heraklion Archaeological Museum, Crete",
   fn:"Cult statues from a sanctuary of Egyptian gods at Gortyn on Crete, where Romans worshipped the rulers of the underworld in Egyptian form.",
   sum:"Hades, the Roman Pluto (“the rich one”), rules the underworld and the dead. He is the oldest brother of Zeus and Poseidon but lives below the earth, so he is usually not counted among the Olympians. Here he stands with a scepter beside his three-headed dog Cerberus, next to his queen Persephone. The pair are dressed as the Egyptian gods Serapis and Isis, showing how Roman religion blended Greek and Egyptian gods.",
   bul:["God of the underworld and the dead","Attributes: scepter, Cerberus, helmet of invisibility","Not an Olympian: lives below"], terms:[]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Reading a god by attributes", img:"h1_artemis", stmt:"Artists name a god by objects and animals, not by a face.",
   para:"Greek and Roman gods look like ideal humans, so a statue of Apollo and one of an athlete can be almost the same body. What tells viewers who is who is the attribute: a thunderbolt for Zeus, a trident for Poseidon, a bow and deer for Artemis, a caduceus for Hermes. Renaissance and later painters kept the same system, so the code still works across two thousand years of art.",
   bul:["Gods = ideal human bodies","Identity carried by objects and animals","Same code used in Renaissance and later art"],
   terms:[], works:["h1_zeus","h1_poseidon","h1_artemis","h1_hermes","h1_athena"]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Why Rome kept the Greek gods", img:"h1_zeus", stmt:"Rome matched its own gods to the Greek ones and gave them Roman jobs.",
   para:"Rome came into contact with Greek culture through trade and the Greek cities of southern Italy, and it matched its own deities to the Greek ones: Jupiter to Zeus, Juno to Hera, Minerva to Athena. The Romans kept Greek stories and copied Greek statues, as many works on this site show, but reorganized the gods around the needs of the Roman state. The three most important became the Capitoline Triad, worshipped together on the Capitoline Hill.",
   bul:["Roman gods matched to Greek counterparts","Greek statues copied for Roman settings","State religion centered on the Capitoline Triad"],
   terms:[["Capitoline Triad","Jupiter, Juno, and Minerva, the three chief gods of Roman state religion, honored together in the great temple on Rome’s Capitoline Hill."]],
   works:["h1_zeus","h1_hera","h1_athena"]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 1 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:2, wk:1, title:"The Cultural Context", sub:"The Greek world that produced the myths",
  overview:{img:"h2_throne", cap:["","Throne Room, Palace of Knossos, Crete","Minoan"],
   sum:"Greek myths took shape over two thousand years of history, from the Bronze Age palaces of Crete and Mycenae to the Hellenistic kingdoms that followed Alexander the Great. A rocky, mountainous land pushed the Greeks toward the sea, trade, and independent city-states. The myths also carry older ideas from Mesopotamia, and they were first written down in the Archaic period by poets who looked back on a lost heroic age.",
   bul:["Historical periods: Minoan → Mycenaean → Dark Age → Archaic → Classical → Hellenistic","Mountains and sea → trade, colonies, separate city-states","Near Eastern roots; written down ca. 700 BCE"],
   terms:[["Greek historical periods","Early/Middle Bronze Age (Minoan) 3000–1600 BCE; Late Bronze Age (Mycenaean) 1600–1150; Dark Age 1150–800; Archaic 800–480; Classical 480–323; Hellenistic 323–31 BCE."],["Greek art periods","Geometric 900–700 BCE; Orientalizing 700–600; Archaic 600–480; Early Classical 480–450; High Classical 450–400; Late Classical 400–323; Hellenistic 323–31 BCE."],["Indo-Europeans","Peoples speaking related languages, Greek among them, who spread from Central Asia across Europe; their societies were ranked into rulers and priests, warriors, and food producers."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h2_knossos_b", artist:"", title:"Palace of Knossos, North Entrance", date:"Minoan, ca. 1700–1450 BCE", det:"Knossos, Crete · columns and bull fresco reconstructed by Arthur Evans",
   fn:"The center of a Bronze Age state: royal residence, storehouse, workshop, and ritual space, built without defensive walls.",
   sum:"The Minoans of Crete, named by the archaeologist Arthur Evans after the mythical King Minos, built huge palace complexes with no fortification walls. The maze of rooms at Knossos may lie behind the myth of the Labyrinth, and the bull images that decorated it echo the story of the Minotaur. The Minoans wrote in a script called Linear A that is still undeciphered.",
   bul:["Minoan Crete: palaces without walls","Possible source of Minos, Labyrinth, Minotaur myths","Bull imagery; Linear A script"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_atreus", artist:"", title:"“Treasury of Atreus”", date:"Mycenaean, ca. 1250 BCE", det:"Stone tholos tomb · Mycenae, Greece",
   fn:"A royal tomb: a long open passage (dromos) leads to a domed, beehive-shaped burial chamber (tholos).",
   sum:"The Mycenaeans were the Greek-speaking Bronze Age culture of the mainland, ruling from walled citadels such as Mycenae and Tiryns. Heinrich Schliemann, who excavated here in the 1870s, believed he had found the world of Homer’s king Agamemnon, and this tomb still carries the name of Agamemnon’s father. The Mycenaeans kept palace accounts in Linear B, an early form of Greek, but did not use writing for stories.",
   bul:["Mycenaean Greece: fortified citadels","Linked by tradition to Agamemnon’s family","Linear B used for records, not myth"],
   terms:[["Tholos","A beehive-shaped stone tomb for Mycenaean rulers."],["Dromos","The long entrance passage leading into a tholos tomb."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Bronze Age ruins behind the myths", img:"h2_throne", stmt:"Heroic myths remember, loosely, a real world that had vanished.",
   para:"Around 1150 BCE the Mycenaean palaces collapsed, writing disappeared, and Greece entered a Dark Age. When poets later told stories of Minos, Agamemnon, and Troy, they were describing a grand past they knew only from ruins and inherited songs. Archaeology has since shown that places like Knossos, Mycenae, and Troy were real, even if the stories told about them are not history.",
   bul:["Palace collapse ca. 1150 BCE","Dark Age: loss of writing","Myths set in real, ruined places"],
   terms:[["Dark Age","The period after the Mycenaean collapse (ca. 1150–800 BCE) marked by depopulation and the loss of writing."]],
   works:["h2_knossos_b","h2_atreus"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_krater", artist:"Attributed to the Hirschfeld Workshop", title:"Funerary krater", date:"Late Geometric, ca. 750–735 BCE", det:"Terracotta · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A grave marker for an Athenian aristocrat: liquid offerings could be poured through its base, and its scenes show the funeral itself.",
   sum:"In the Geometric period Greek potters covered vases with bands of zigzags and meanders, and then added stick-like human figures. This huge krater shows the dead man laid out on a bier surrounded by mourners, with a procession of chariots and warriors below. Scenes like these appear just as the Greek alphabet was being adopted, around the time Homer’s poems took shape.",
   bul:["Geometric style: bands of pattern, stick figures","Funeral scene and chariot procession","Era of the new Greek alphabet"],
   terms:[["Krater","A large bowl for mixing wine with water; giant ones served as grave markers in the Geometric period."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_kouros", artist:"", title:"Marble statue of a kouros (youth)", date:"Archaic, ca. 590–580 BCE", det:"Marble · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A grave marker for a young Athenian man, showing him as an ideal youth rather than a portrait.",
   sum:"Kouroi (singular kouros) are standing nude youths, left foot forward and arms at their sides, a pose borrowed from Egyptian sculpture. This is one of the earliest surviving Athenian marble statues. Over the next century Greek sculptors gradually loosened this stiff pose into the natural, balanced bodies of the Classical period, a change that happened unusually fast.",
   bul:["Egyptian-inspired pose, left foot forward","Ideal youth, not a portrait","Start of fast change toward Classical naturalism"],
   terms:[["Kouros","An Archaic Greek statue of a standing nude young man, used as a grave marker or an offering to a god."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_hoplite", artist:"", title:"Neck-amphora with a Greek warrior and a Persian", date:"Athenian, early 5th century BCE", det:"Red-figure terracotta · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A storage jar for wine or oil; scenes of Greeks defeating Persians were popular after the Persian Wars.",
   sum:"A Greek hoplite with a round shield and spear attacks a Persian archer in trousers. Between 490 and 479 BCE the Greek city-states defeated two Persian invasions at battles including Marathon, Thermopylae, and Salamis. The victory gave Greeks a shared identity defined against “barbarian” Asia, an idea that runs through Classical art and its use of myth.",
   bul:["Persian Wars, 490–479 BCE","Greek vs. Persian = civilization vs. “other”","Hoplite armor: helmet, round shield, spear"],
   terms:[["Hoplite","A heavily armed Greek citizen-soldier who fought shoulder to shoulder in a phalanx, replacing the one-on-one duels of Homeric heroes."],["Hellenes","The Greeks’ own name for themselves, from Hellas; “Greek” comes from the Romans."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_leonidas", artist:"Jacques-Louis David", title:"Leonidas at Thermopylae", date:"1814", det:"Oil on canvas · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A large history painting meant to show patriotic self-sacrifice, finished in the last months of Napoleon’s empire.",
   sum:"In 480 BCE King Leonidas of Sparta and a small force held the narrow pass of Thermopylae against the Persian army before being killed. David shows Leonidas calmly seated among his men just before the final battle. The painting shows how later Europe treated Greek history itself almost as myth, a story of heroic virtue.",
   bul:["Thermopylae, 480 BCE","Spartan sacrifice as heroic model","Greek history used as moral example"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_pericles", artist:"After Kresilas", title:"Bust of Pericles", date:"Roman copy of a Greek original of ca. 430 BCE", det:"Marble · Vatican Museums (Museo Chiaramonti)",
   fn:"A Roman copy of the honorary portrait set up on the Athenian Acropolis.",
   sum:"Pericles led Athens at its height in the mid-400s BCE, when the Parthenon was built and drama, history, and philosophy flourished. He wears a general’s helmet, pushed up to show his face. Athens was a democracy, but only free adult male citizens could vote, and the city’s wealth rested partly on slaves and on an empire of allied cities.",
   bul:["Leader of Classical Athens","Helmet = office of general","Democracy limited to male citizens"],
   terms:[["Polis (pl. poleis)","An independent Greek city-state with its own laws, army, and gods, such as Athens or Corinth."],["Demos","The citizen body of a polis: free men who owned property, spoke and voted in the assembly, and fought in its wars."],["Tyrants","Rulers who took power outside the old aristocracy (ca. 650–500 BCE), often with commercial backing; the bad sense of the word comes from aristocratic writers."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_alexmosaic", artist:"", title:"Alexander Mosaic (Battle of Issus)", date:"Roman, ca. 100 BCE, after a Greek painting of ca. 315 BCE", det:"Floor mosaic from the House of the Faun, Pompeii · National Archaeological Museum, Naples",
   fn:"A floor mosaic of more than a million tiny stones made for one of the grandest houses in Pompeii, copying a lost Greek painting.",
   sum:"Alexander III of Macedon (356–323 BCE) conquered the Persian Empire and carried Greek culture to Egypt and India. At left he charges bareheaded on his horse Bucephalus, while the Persian king Darius turns in his chariot to flee. Alexander’s death in 323 BCE begins the Hellenistic period, when Greek kingdoms spread Greek art and myth across the eastern Mediterranean.",
   bul:["Conquered Persia, 334–323 BCE","Alexander vs. Darius at Issus (333 BCE)","Start of the Hellenistic period"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_symposium", artist:"Brygos Painter", title:"Symposium scene", date:"Athenian, ca. 490–480 BCE", det:"Red-figure cup (interior) · British Museum, London",
   fn:"A drinking cup used at a symposium; the picture inside was revealed as the drinker emptied it.",
   sum:"A reclining young man hands a pair of pipes to a female musician. The symposium was an all-male evening of drinking, poetry, song, and conversation, and it was one of the main places where myths were retold and where painted cups showing them were used. The women present were paid entertainers, not wives.",
   bul:["All-male drinking party","Setting for poetry, song, and myth","Cups decorated for this use"],
   terms:[["Symposium","An all-male drinking party with poetry, music, and talk; a key setting for retelling myths."],["Hetairai","Educated female companions paid to entertain men with music and conversation at symposia."],["Aoidoi, rhapsodes","Aoidoi were singer-poets who composed myths in performance; rhapsodes later recited memorized epic poetry."],["Pedagogue","The enslaved attendant who supervised a boy’s schooling, roughly from age 6 to 13."],["Pederasty","A socially recognized relationship between an adult citizen man and an adolescent boy, framed as mentorship, common in aristocratic Athens."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_prothesis", artist:"Dipylon Master", title:"Krater fragment with a prothesis", date:"Late Geometric, ca. 760–750 BCE", det:"Terracotta · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"Part of a grave-marker vase from Athens’ Dipylon cemetery; the prothesis was the laying-out of the body for mourning.",
   sum:"Women lead the mourning around the dead, raising their hands to their heads. Greek women spent most of their lives in the household, but birth and death, the two events that brought ritual pollution, were their responsibility. The myths repeatedly define women by the stages of their lives, from unmarried girl to bride to mother.",
   bul:["Women lead the mourning","Birth and death handled by women","Life stages: girl → bride → mother"],
   terms:[["Gynaikeion","The women’s quarters of a Greek house, where women spun and wove and young children were raised."],["Parthenos","An unmarried girl of marriageable age, linked with the wild goddess Artemis."],["Nymphe","A bride or newly married woman."],["Gyne","A grown, married woman, a status fully reached through childbirth."],["Miasma","Ritual pollution caused by events such as birth, death, and bloodshed, which required purification."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Myth in a man’s world", img:"h2_symposium", stmt:"Most of what survives was written by and for elite men.",
   para:"Almost all of our sources come from aristocratic men in Athens in the 400s and 300s BCE. Myths were performed at their symposia, paid for by their families, and shaped by their views of war, honor, and women. Reading the myths with this in mind helps explain why heroes are male warriors and why women in the stories are so often dangerous, silent, or blamed.",
   bul:["Sources: elite Athenian men","Performed at symposia and festivals","Shapes how women appear in myth"],
   terms:[], works:["h2_symposium","h2_prothesis"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_uruk", artist:"", title:"Female head from Uruk (possibly Inanna)", date:"Sumerian, ca. 3200–3000 BCE", det:"Marble · Iraq Museum, Baghdad",
   fn:"Probably the face of a cult statue from the temple precinct of Inanna at Uruk; the eyes and brows once held colored inlays.",
   sum:"Each Sumerian city had its own protecting god, who lived in a temple at the city’s center. Inanna, the “Queen of Heaven,” was the goddess of love and war, and Greek writers’ Aphrodite shares some of her traits. Many of the patterns of Greek myth, such as generations of gods and a great flood, appear first in Mesopotamia.",
   bul:["Sumerian city gods","Inanna: love and war","Mesopotamian roots of Greek myth"],
   terms:[["Sumerian gods","An (sky, first ruler), Inanna (love and war), Enlil (storms and rule), Enki (fresh water and wisdom), Ki (earth), and Ereshkigal (queen of the underworld)."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h2_naramsin", artist:"", title:"Victory Stele of Naram-Sin", date:"Akkadian, ca. 2254–2218 BCE", det:"Sandstone · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A public monument celebrating a royal victory; it was carried off to Susa as war booty more than a thousand years later.",
   sum:"The Akkadian king Naram-Sin climbs a mountain over defeated enemies, wearing the horned crown that marks a god. When the Semitic-speaking Akkadians conquered Sumer around 2300 BCE, they took over the Sumerian gods under new names and passed them to the Babylonians. That chain of borrowing is how Near Eastern stories reached the Greeks.",
   bul:["King shown with a god’s horned crown","Akkadians adopted Sumerian gods","Ideas passed on to Babylon and beyond"], terms:[]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Myth’s roots in the Near East", img:"h2_uruk", stmt:"Greek myth grew out of much older Mesopotamian traditions.",
   para:"Sumerians, Akkadians, and Babylonians told of gods with human passions, generations of gods overthrowing one another, and a flood sent to destroy humankind. Greek stories borrowed and reshaped these patterns, just as Rome would later borrow from Greece. The Hebrew Bible draws on the same Mesopotamian stock, which is why Genesis and Greek myth sometimes look alike.",
   bul:["Sumer → Akkad → Babylon → Greece","Shared patterns: divine generations, flood","Genesis draws on the same sources"],
   terms:[], works:["h2_uruk","h2_naramsin","h4_flood"]},
  {type:"text", title:"Where the stories come from", sub:"The main written sources",
   sum:"Greek myths were told for centuries before Homer and Hesiod wrote versions down around 700 BCE. Athenian tragedy reworked them for the stage in the 400s BCE, and Roman poets gave them their most influential forms. The works below are the main sources later artists read.",
   bul:["Homer and Hesiod: earliest written versions (ca. 700 BCE)","Tragedy: myth staged in Classical Athens","Virgil and Ovid: the Roman retellings artists used most"],
   terms:[["Homer, Iliad and Odyssey","Epic poems; the Iliad covers weeks in the tenth year of the Trojan War, the Odyssey Odysseus’s ten-year voyage home."],["Hesiod, Theogony and Works and Days","The Theogony tells the birth of the gods and Zeus’s rise; Works and Days gives advice on life and includes Prometheus, Pandora, and the Five Ages."],["Tragedy: Aeschylus, Sophocles, Euripides","Athenian playwrights who staged myths at festivals of Dionysus; Aeschylus stresses the gods, Sophocles fate, Euripides human passion."],["Virgil, Aeneid (29–19 BCE)","Roman epic written under Augustus, tracing Aeneas from fallen Troy to Italy and Rome’s founding."],["Ovid, Metamorphoses (8 CE)","A long Roman poem retelling hundreds of myths of transformation; the single most-used source for later artists."]]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 2 · PART 1 ================= */
 {n:3, wk:2, title:"Creation and the Rise of Zeus", sub:"From Chaos to the rule of the Olympians",
  overview:{img:"h3_titans", cap:["Cornelis van Haarlem","The Fall of the Titans","1588–90"],
   sum:"Hesiod’s Theogony (“birth of the gods”) tells how the world began with Chaos and how three generations of gods fought for control of it. Sky-god Uranus is overthrown by his son Cronus, and Cronus by his son Zeus. Zeus then has to defeat the Titans, the monster Typhon, and the Giants before the Olympians rule for good and divide the world among themselves.",
   bul:["Chaos → Earth → Sky → Titans → Olympians","Three cosmic wars secure Zeus’s rule","World divided: sky, sea, underworld"],
   terms:[["Chaos","The first thing to exist in Hesiod: a yawning gap or void, not disorder."],["Gaea (Gaia)","Earth, who comes from Chaos and gives birth to the Sky and the Sea, then mother of the Titans and many monsters."],["Uranus","The Sky, son and husband of Gaea; overthrown by his son Cronus."],["Tartarus","The deepest pit beneath the earth, where defeated gods and monsters are locked away."]]},
  quote:{q:"Verily at the first Chaos came to be, but next wide-bosomed Earth", by:"Hesiod, Theogony 116–117, trans. Hugh G. Evelyn-White (1914)"},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h3_uranus", artist:"Giorgio Vasari and Cristofano Gherardi", title:"The Mutilation of Uranus by Saturn", date:"ca. 1556", det:"Ceiling painting · Sala degli Elementi, Palazzo Vecchio, Florence",
   fn:"Part of a ceiling program for Duke Cosimo I de’ Medici’s palace apartments, using myth to celebrate the elements and, indirectly, the duke.",
   sum:"Uranus hated his children and kept them hidden inside Earth, so Gaea made a great sickle and her youngest Titan son, Cronus, ambushed and castrated his father. Blood falling on the earth produced the Furies and the Giants, and the severed parts thrown into the sea produced Aphrodite. Vasari’s ceiling turns this violent episode into the origin of the elements.",
   bul:["Gaea’s sickle; Cronus attacks Uranus","Blood → Furies, Giants","Sea foam → Aphrodite"],
   terms:[["Titans","The twelve children of Gaea and Uranus, including Oceanus, Hyperion, Cronus, and Rhea; the generation of gods before the Olympians."],["Erinyes (Furies)","Female spirits of vengeance born from Uranus’s blood, who hunt down those who shed family blood."],["Giants","Huge, violent sons of Earth born from Uranus’s blood, later defeated by the Olympians."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_venus", artist:"Alexandre Cabanel", title:"The Birth of Venus", date:"1875", det:"Oil on canvas · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A smaller version of Cabanel’s famous 1863 Paris Salon painting, made for an American collector.",
   sum:"In Hesiod’s account, Aphrodite is born from the foam that gathered around Uranus’s severed genitals, which is how ancient writers explained her name (aphros means “foam”). Cabanel turns the story into a dreamy academic nude floating on the waves with cupids overhead. The original version was bought by Napoleon III, showing how mythology gave respectable cover to the nude in 19th-century art.",
   bul:["Born from sea foam (aphros)","Academic Salon painting","Myth as cover for the nude"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_phaeton", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"The Fall of Phaeton", date:"ca. 1604–08", det:"Oil on canvas · National Gallery of Art, Washington",
   fn:"An early large mythological painting from Rubens’s years in Rome; its first owner is not recorded.",
   sum:"The Titan Hyperion fathered the sun (Helios), the moon (Selene), and the dawn (Eos). Helios’s son Phaeton begged to drive the sun’s chariot for one day, lost control, and scorched the earth until Zeus struck him down with a thunderbolt. Ancient writers used the story to explain deserts and why some peoples had dark skin. Rubens shows the moment the horses scatter and the hours of the day flee in panic.",
   bul:["Hyperion → Helios, Selene, Eos","Phaeton loses the sun chariot","Etiological story of scorched lands"],
   terms:[["Hyperion","A Titan, father of the sun Helios, the moon Selene, and the dawn Eos."],["Phaeton","Son of Helios who wrecked the sun’s chariot and was killed by Zeus’s thunderbolt."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_saturn", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"Saturn", date:"1636–38", det:"Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid",
   fn:"One of dozens of mythological paintings commissioned by Philip IV of Spain for his hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada.",
   sum:"Cronus, the Roman Saturn, learned that one of his children would overthrow him, so he swallowed each one at birth. His wife Rhea saved the youngest, Zeus, by hiding him on Crete and giving Cronus a stone wrapped in swaddling clothes. Rubens paints an old man biting into a child’s chest, with the stars of the planet Saturn above. Renaissance writers confused Cronus with Chronos, “Time,” which is why he often carries a scythe and appears as Father Time.",
   bul:["Cronus swallows his children","Rhea substitutes a stone for Zeus","Confused with Time (Chronos)"],
   terms:[["Cronus (Kronos)","Youngest Titan, who overthrew Uranus and swallowed his own children to keep power."],["Rhea","Titaness, wife of Cronus and mother of Hestia, Demeter, Hera, Hades, Poseidon, and Zeus."]],
   quote:{q:"These great Cronos swallowed as each came forth from the womb to his mother’s knees", by:"Hesiod, Theogony 459–460, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"theme", title:"Sons overthrow fathers", img:"h3_goya", stmt:"Power passes by violence from one generation to the next.",
   para:"Uranus suppresses his children and is overthrown by Cronus; Cronus swallows his children and is overthrown by Zeus. Zeus breaks the cycle by swallowing his first wife Metis before she can bear a son greater than he is, and Athena is born from his head instead. Goya’s later painting of Saturn, made on the wall of his own house, shows how this ancient story could become an image of pure terror.",
   bul:["Uranus → Cronus → Zeus","Each father fears a son","Zeus ends the cycle by swallowing Metis"],
   terms:[["Metis","Titaness of cleverness, Zeus’s first wife, swallowed by him while pregnant with Athena."]],
   works:["h3_uranus","h3_saturn","h3_athena"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_titans", artist:"Cornelis van Haarlem", title:"The Fall of the Titans", date:"1588–90", det:"Oil on canvas · Statens Museum for Kunst, Copenhagen",
   fn:"A showpiece of Dutch Mannerism, made to display the painter’s skill with twisting, muscular nudes.",
   sum:"The Titanomachy was a ten-year war between the Titans and Zeus’s Olympians. Zeus won only after freeing the Cyclopes, who forged his thunderbolt, and the Hundred-Handers, who hurled boulders. The defeated Titans were locked in Tartarus, and Atlas was condemned to hold up the sky. Van Haarlem shows the Titans tumbling down in a tangle of bodies.",
   bul:["Titanomachy: ten-year war","Cyclopes and Hundred-Handers help Zeus","Losers imprisoned in Tartarus"],
   terms:[["Titanomachy","The war between the Titans and the Olympians that made Zeus king."],["Cyclopes","Three one-eyed sons of Gaea and Uranus, smiths who forged Zeus’s thunderbolt."],["Hecatonchires (Hundred-Handers)","Three giants with fifty heads and a hundred arms each who threw boulders at the Titans."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_atlas", artist:"", title:"Farnese Atlas", date:"Roman, 2nd century CE, copy of an earlier original", det:"Marble · National Archaeological Museum, Naples",
   fn:"A Roman statue that doubles as a sky map: the globe on Atlas’s shoulders is carved with the constellations.",
   sum:"Atlas, a Titan who fought against Zeus, was punished by being made to hold up the heavens at the western edge of the world. The sculptor shows him bent under a globe carved with the constellations, one of the oldest surviving images of the star groups. His name lives on in the word for a book of maps.",
   bul:["Titan punished after the Titanomachy","Holds up the sky","Globe carved with constellations"],
   terms:[["Atlas","Titan condemned by Zeus to hold the sky on his shoulders forever."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_chimera", artist:"", title:"Chimera of Arezzo", date:"Etruscan, ca. 400 BCE", det:"Bronze · National Archaeological Museum, Florence",
   fn:"An offering to the Etruscan sky god (an inscription on its leg reads tinscvil, “gift to Tinia”), discovered at Arezzo in 1553.",
   sum:"Gaea also mated with Pontus, the sea, and her descendants include a line of monsters. The Chimera breathed fire and had a lion’s body, a goat’s head rising from its back, and a snake for a tail; the hero Bellerophon killed it while riding the winged horse Pegasus. This bronze shows the wounded beast snarling, and the goat’s head is already bleeding.",
   bul:["Lion, goat, and serpent in one body","Killed by Bellerophon on Pegasus","Etruscan votive bronze"],
   terms:[["Pontus","The sea, born from Gaea alone; through his children she becomes ancestor of many monsters."],["Chimera","Fire-breathing monster, part lion, part goat, part serpent, killed by Bellerophon."],["Gorgons","Three snake-haired sisters, including Medusa, whose gaze turned people to stone."],["Geryon","A three-bodied giant whose cattle Heracles stole, guarded by Orthus."],["Orthus","Geryon’s two-headed guard dog."],["Cerberus","The many-headed dog guarding the gate of the underworld."],["Hydra","Many-headed water serpent that grew new heads when one was cut off, killed by Heracles."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Monsters with borrowed bodies", img:"h3_typhon", stmt:"Greek monsters mix animal and human parts, an idea borrowed from older art.",
   para:"Egyptian and Near Eastern art had long combined human and animal parts, often for protective gods. Greek artists borrowed these hybrids, like the sphinx, the griffin, and the winged Harpies, but usually made them threats to be destroyed. Killing a monster became the defining act of a hero, and the monsters stand for the wild disorder that civilization must overcome.",
   bul:["Hybrids borrowed from Egypt and the Near East","Protective images become threats","Heroes prove themselves by killing them"],
   terms:[], works:["h3_chimera","h3_typhon"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_athena", artist:"", title:"Amphora with the birth of Athena", date:"Athenian, ca. 550–525 BCE", det:"Black-figure terracotta · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"A storage jar for wine or oil, painted with a popular myth for an Athenian buyer; Athena was the city’s goddess.",
   sum:"Zeus swallowed his pregnant wife Metis, and later, suffering a terrible headache, had his head split open with an axe, usually by Hephaestus. Out sprang Athena, fully grown and armed. The painter shows the tiny armored goddess emerging above the seated Zeus. Black-figure painters painted figures in black slip and scratched in details, leaving the red clay as background.",
   bul:["Athena born from Zeus’s head","Hephaestus’s axe splits the skull","Black-figure technique"],
   terms:[["Black-figure","Greek vase technique (ca. 700–500 BCE) with figures in black slip and incised details on the red clay."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_typhon", artist:"", title:"Hydria with Zeus fighting Typhon", date:"Chalcidian, ca. 540–530 BCE", det:"Black-figure terracotta · Staatliche Antikensammlungen, Munich",
   fn:"A hydria, a three-handled jar used to carry water; this one, made by Greek potters in southern Italy, was found in an Etruscan grave.",
   sum:"Angry at the defeat of her Titan children, Gaea mated with Tartarus and gave birth to Typhon, a winged giant with a hundred snake heads. In the Typhonomachy Zeus fought him with thunderbolts and finally buried him under Mount Etna in Sicily, whose eruptions were said to be the monster’s fire. Here Zeus strides forward with a thunderbolt at the winged, snake-legged Typhon.",
   bul:["Typhon = son of Gaea and Tartarus","Typhonomachy: Zeus vs. Typhon","Buried under Etna: why volcanoes erupt"],
   terms:[["Typhonomachy","Zeus’s battle with the monster Typhon, the second fight for control of the world."],["Typhon (Typhoeus)","A monstrous, winged, snake-legged son of Gaea and Tartarus, defeated by Zeus."]],
   quote:{q:"And in the bitterness of his anger Zeus cast him into wide Tartarus.", by:"Hesiod, Theogony 868, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_perg_c", artist:"", title:"Pergamon Altar: Athena fighting the Giant Alcyoneus", date:"Hellenistic, ca. 180–160 BCE", det:"Marble relief · Pergamon Museum, Berlin",
   fn:"Part of the frieze wrapping a monumental altar built by the kings of Pergamon, who compared their victories over the Gauls to the gods’ victory over the Giants.",
   sum:"The Gigantomachy was the last great battle, in which the Olympians, with the help of the mortal hero Heracles, defeated the Giants. On this frieze Athena grabs the winged Giant Alcyoneus by the hair, while his mother Gaea rises from the ground pleading for him and Victory flies in to crown the goddess. The writhing, deeply carved bodies are typical of Hellenistic drama.",
   bul:["Gigantomachy: gods vs. Giants","Gaea pleads for her son","Political message for Pergamon’s kings"],
   terms:[["Gigantomachy","The war of the Olympians against the Giants, often used in art as a symbol of order defeating barbarism."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h3_giulio", artist:"Giulio Romano", title:"Sala dei Giganti (Room of the Giants)", date:"1532–35", det:"Fresco · Palazzo Te, Mantua",
   fn:"A room in the pleasure palace of Federico II Gonzaga, Duke of Mantua, painted on every wall and the ceiling to overwhelm visitors.",
   sum:"Giulio Romano painted the Giants crushed under collapsing columns and mountains as Jupiter hurls thunderbolts from the dome above. Because the painting covers the whole room, visitors feel the building falling around them. The theme flattered Federico’s patron, the Emperor Charles V, as a Jupiter who crushes rebels.",
   bul:["Whole room painted floor to ceiling","Jupiter crushes the Giants","Political flattery of Charles V"], terms:[]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Order defeats chaos", img:"h3_perg_c", stmt:"Each cosmic war replaces raw force with Zeus’s order.",
   para:"The Titans, Typhon, and the Giants are all children of Earth who stand for wild, unchecked power. Zeus wins by alliances and intelligence as much as by force, then shares out the world: he takes the sky, Poseidon the sea, and Hades the underworld. Later rulers, from the kings of Pergamon to Renaissance princes, used these battles to cast their own enemies as monsters.",
   bul:["Three wars: Titans, Typhon, Giants","Zeus shares the world by lot","Reused as political propaganda"],
   terms:[["Division of the world","After victory, Zeus, Poseidon, and Hades cast lots: Zeus took the sky, Poseidon the sea, Hades the underworld; earth and Olympus were shared."]],
   works:["h3_titans","h3_typhon","h3_perg_c","h3_giulio"]},
  {type:"text", title:"Another way to begin", sub:"A creation story from a cosmic egg",
   sum:"Hesiod’s was not the only Greek creation story. A version preserved in Aristophanes’ comedy Birds (414 BCE) and in later Orphic poems begins with Night (Nyx), pictured as a black-winged bird, who lays an egg in the darkness. From it hatches Eros, the force of love, who brings the other elements together; Homer, meanwhile, calls Oceanus the origin of the gods.",
   bul:["Night lays a cosmic egg","Eros hatches first and joins things together","Several Greek origin stories existed side by side"],
   terms:[["Nyx","Night, one of the first beings from Chaos; in the egg story, the black-winged mother of Eros."],["Eros","Desire, one of the oldest powers in Hesiod and the first-born in the egg story; later the boy Cupid."],["Oceanus","Titan of the great river encircling the earth, called the origin of the gods in Homer’s Iliad."]]}
  ]},

 /* ================= WEEK 2 · PART 2 ================= */
 {n:4, wk:2, title:"The Origins of Mortals", sub:"Prometheus, Pandora, the Five Ages, and the Flood",
  overview:{img:"h4_sarc", cap:["","Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind","Roman, ca. 240 CE"],
   sum:"The Greeks had no single story of how humans were made; Hesiod never says. Later writers credit the Titan Prometheus with shaping people from clay, and every version agrees that he became humankind’s champion against Zeus. His tricks cost humans dearly: Zeus answered with Pandora, the first woman, and the stories go on to explain toil, sickness, marriage, and a long decline from a golden age.",
   bul:["Prometheus shapes and protects humans","Zeus’s answer: Pandora and her jar","Decline through five ages; the flood"],
   terms:[["Prometheus","Titan whose name means “forethought”; trickster who sides with humans, steals fire, and is punished by Zeus."],["Epimetheus","Prometheus’s brother, “afterthought,” who accepts Pandora despite being warned against gifts from Zeus."]]},
  slides:[
  {type:"work", key:"h4_piero", artist:"Piero di Cosimo", title:"The Myth of Prometheus", date:"ca. 1510–15", det:"Oil on poplar panel · Alte Pinakothek, Munich",
   fn:"One of a pair of long panels (the other is in Strasbourg) made as part of the furnishings of a room in a Florentine home.",
   sum:"Piero tells the story as a sequence: Prometheus shapes a clay statue of a man, flies up with Athena to light a torch from the sun’s chariot, and brings the fire down to give the figure life. In this version, drawn from later writers, Prometheus is less a trickster than a creator, an idea Renaissance thinkers compared to God shaping Adam.",
   bul:["Continuous narrative: one panel, several moments","Prometheus as maker of humans","Fire from the sun animates the figure"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_sarc", artist:"", title:"Sarcophagus with Prometheus creating humankind", date:"Roman, ca. 240 CE", det:"Marble · Louvre, Paris; on deposit at the Musée départemental Arles antique since 2022",
   fn:"A coffin carved for a wealthy Roman and found in the Alyscamps cemetery at Arles; the creation and life of humans framed the death of the person inside.",
   sum:"Prometheus sits modeling a small human figure while gods watch, among them Athena in her helmet and Hermes in his winged hat. In the Roman versions known from Ovid and later writers, Prometheus mixes earth and water to make people in the gods’ image, and Athena gives them reason. Christian writers later saw a pagan parallel to God forming Adam from earth in Genesis.",
   bul:["Prometheus models a human figure","Athena gives understanding","Parallel to Genesis"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_kylix", artist:"Arkesilas Painter", title:"Cup with Atlas and Prometheus", date:"Laconian (Spartan), ca. 560–550 BCE", det:"Black-figure terracotta · Vatican Museums (Museo Gregoriano Etrusco)",
   fn:"A drinking cup from Sparta, found in Etruria; the picture filled the inside of the bowl.",
   sum:"Two brothers suffer side by side: Atlas holds up the sky, and Prometheus, bound to a post, is attacked by Zeus’s eagle, which eats his liver every day while it grows back each night. This is one of the earliest images of the myth. In Hesiod the hero Heracles later kills the eagle and frees Prometheus, with Zeus’s consent.",
   bul:["Earliest images of the punishment","Eagle eats a regrowing liver","Atlas and Prometheus are brothers"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_cossiers", artist:"Jan Cossiers, after a sketch by Rubens", title:"Prometheus Carrying Fire", date:"1636–38", det:"Oil on canvas · Museo del Prado, Madrid",
   fn:"Painted for Philip IV’s hunting lodge, the Torre de la Parada, as part of Rubens’s workshop series on Ovid’s myths.",
   sum:"At a sacrifice at Mecone, Prometheus hid the good meat of an ox inside its unappetizing stomach and wrapped the bones in shining fat, and Zeus chose the fat. That trick explained why Greeks burned bones and fat for the gods and ate the meat themselves. Zeus retaliated by hiding fire, and Prometheus stole it back in a hollow fennel stalk, the burning torch he runs with here.",
   bul:["Trick at Mecone: bones in fat","Etiology of Greek sacrifice","Fire stolen in a fennel stalk"], terms:[],
   quote:{q:"stole again for men from Zeus the counsellor in a hollow fennel-stalk", by:"Hesiod, Works and Days 50–52, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_baburen", artist:"Dirck van Baburen", title:"Prometheus Being Chained by Vulcan", date:"1623", det:"Oil on canvas · Rijksmuseum, Amsterdam",
   fn:"A dramatic gallery painting by a Utrecht follower of Caravaggio; no patron or first owner is recorded.",
   sum:"Vulcan, the smith, hammers chains onto Prometheus while Mercury points to the punishment. Baburen borrows Caravaggio’s strong light and dark and his plain, physical bodies, so the scene feels like a real execution. By this time Vulcan stood for technology itself, and Mercury for cunning, the same qualities that got Prometheus into trouble.",
   bul:["Vulcan chains, Mercury oversees","Caravaggesque light and realism","Technology punishing its giver"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_rubens", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens, eagle by Frans Snyders", title:"Prometheus Bound", date:"1611–18", det:"Oil on canvas · Philadelphia Museum of Art",
   fn:"A large showpiece Rubens kept for years and later sold; he chose the subject to show his mastery of the heroic nude.",
   sum:"Rubens shows Prometheus falling headfirst down the rocks as the eagle tears at him, a pose built from his study of ancient sculpture and Michelangelo. In Aeschylus’s play Prometheus Bound, the Titan defends himself by listing what he taught humans: building, counting, writing, medicine, and more. That made him a hero of human progress for later artists and writers.",
   bul:["Collaboration with Snyders (eagle)","Pose from ancient sculpture and Michelangelo","Aeschylus: benefactor of civilization"], terms:[]},
  {type:"theme", title:"The trickster who made us human", img:"h4_kylix", stmt:"Every gift from Prometheus comes with a cost to humans.",
   para:"Prometheus’s trick at the sacrifice gave humans the meat but left the gods the smoke, setting people halfway between gods and animals. Stealing fire gave them technology but brought on Zeus’s anger and Pandora. The story explains why human life mixes progress with suffering, and later ages turned Prometheus into a symbol of creativity, rebellion, and the price of knowledge.",
   bul:["Sacrifice places humans between gods and beasts","Fire = technology and punishment","Later symbol of creativity and rebellion"],
   terms:[], works:["h4_cossiers","h4_baburen","h4_rubens","h4_kylix"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_epimetheus", artist:"Giulio Bonasone", title:"Epimetheus Opening Pandora’s Vase", date:"mid-16th century", det:"Engraving · Metropolitan Museum of Art, New York",
   fn:"A print sold to collectors and artists; prints like this spread mythological imagery across Europe.",
   sum:"To punish humans for the stolen fire, Zeus had Hephaestus shape the first woman from earth and water; Athena taught her weaving, Aphrodite gave her desire, and Hermes gave her a deceitful mind. She was called Pandora, “all gifts,” and was presented to Epimetheus. Bonasone’s print is unusual in showing Epimetheus himself lifting the lid as the evils fly out.",
   bul:["Pandora made by Hephaestus, gifted by the gods","Presented to Epimetheus","Here Epimetheus opens the vessel"],
   terms:[["Pandora","The first woman in Hesiod, “all gifts,” made by the gods to punish humans for Prometheus’s theft."]],
   quote:{q:"the very famous Limping God formed of earth the likeness of a shy maiden", by:"Hesiod, Theogony 571–572, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_cousin", artist:"Jean Cousin the Elder", title:"Eva Prima Pandora", date:"ca. 1550", det:"Oil on panel · Louvre, Paris",
   fn:"The Louvre acquired it in 1922 from Sens, Cousin’s home town; the title, “Eve the first Pandora,” spells out its argument.",
   sum:"Cousin merges two first women: Eve of Genesis and Pandora of Greek myth. She reclines with a skull under her arm, an apple branch, and a jar, while a snake curls around her arm. Both stories blame a woman’s curiosity for bringing suffering into the world, and Renaissance thinkers liked to treat pagan myths as echoes of the Bible.",
   bul:["Eve and Pandora combined","Skull, apple, jar, snake","Both blame a woman for human suffering"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_waterhouse", artist:"John William Waterhouse", title:"Pandora", date:"1896", det:"Oil on canvas · private collection",
   fn:"A Victorian exhibition painting for a public that loved romantic, literary subjects.",
   sum:"Waterhouse shows Pandora kneeling in a wood, peering into a golden box as a wisp of smoke escapes. In Hesiod the container is a large storage jar (pithos); it became a “box” through a Renaissance mistranslation, and later versions add the rule that she must not open it. When she lifts the lid, sickness and toil escape, and only Hope stays inside.",
   bul:["Jar (pithos), not a box","Evils escape; Hope remains","Romantic Victorian version"],
   terms:[["Pithos","A large clay storage jar; the original vessel in Hesiod, later called a “box.”"]],
   quote:{q:"Only Hope remained there in an unbreakable home within under the rim of the great jar", by:"Hesiod, Works and Days 96–97, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_danaides", artist:"John William Waterhouse", title:"The Danaïdes", date:"1903", det:"Oil on canvas · private collection",
   fn:"A Victorian exhibition painting of a punishment in the underworld.",
   sum:"The fifty daughters of Danaus were forced to marry their cousins, and all but one killed her husband on the wedding night. In the underworld they were sentenced to carry water forever in leaking jars that never fill. Waterhouse paints them pouring endlessly into a basin. The jar, like Pandora’s, links women with containers, and Greek ideas of marriage cast the bride as a vessel for children.",
   bul:["Killed their husbands on the wedding night","Endless water-carrying in Hades","Jar as symbol of the female body"],
   terms:[["Danaïds","The daughters of Danaus, punished in the underworld for murdering their husbands by carrying water in leaking jars."],["Marriage = children = immortality","Greek belief that, with no happy afterlife for most people, a person lived on through children, which made marriage necessary."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"Woman as a container", img:"h4_cousin", stmt:"Greek myth repeatedly pictures women as vessels.",
   para:"Pandora comes with a jar, the Danaïds carry broken jars, and Greek women in daily life carried water jars and wedding gifts. Writers compared a woman to a jar or a stomach: something that holds and consumes. Hesiod treats women as a costly necessity, since a man without children has no one to care for him in old age. These stories say more about Greek men’s anxieties than about real women.",
   bul:["Jar = womb, storage, and burial urn","Marriage resented but necessary","Myth reflects male anxieties"],
   terms:[], works:["h4_epimetheus","h4_cousin","h4_waterhouse","h4_danaides"]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_cranach", artist:"Lucas Cranach the Elder", title:"The Golden Age", date:"ca. 1530", det:"Oil on panel · Nasjonalmuseet, Oslo",
   fn:"An oak panel picturing a mythical paradise; its original owner is not recorded.",
   sum:"Hesiod describes five races of humans, each worse than the last. In the Golden Age under Cronus, people lived like gods, without work, illness, or old age, and died as if falling asleep. Cranach paints them dancing and bathing in a walled garden, an image close to the Christian Eden. The silver race was foolish, the bronze violent, and the heroes better, but Hesiod’s own Iron Age is the worst of all.",
   bul:["Gold → Silver → Bronze → Heroes → Iron","Golden Age: no toil, no old age","Hesiod lives in the Iron Age"],
   terms:[["Five Ages (Races)","Hesiod’s history of humankind: Golden, Silver, Bronze, the Age of Heroes, and the present Iron Age."]],
   quote:{q:"they lived like gods without sorrow of heart, remote and free from toil and grief", by:"Hesiod, Works and Days 112–113, trans. Evelyn-White"}},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_flood", artist:"", title:"The Flood Tablet (Epic of Gilgamesh, Tablet XI)", date:"Neo-Assyrian, 7th century BCE", det:"Clay · British Museum, London",
   fn:"A tablet from the library of the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal at Nineveh, a royal collection of literature and learning.",
   sum:"When George Smith of the British Museum translated this tablet in 1872, he found a flood story strikingly like Noah’s: the gods decide to destroy humanity, one god warns a good man to build a boat, and he releases birds to find land. Older Sumerian and Akkadian versions name the hero Ziusudra and Atrahasis. The Greek flood story belongs to the same family.",
   bul:["Translated by George Smith, 1872","Boat, warning god, birds sent out","Mesopotamian flood stories predate Genesis"],
   terms:[["Ziusudra and Enki","In the Sumerian flood story, the god Enki warns the good king Ziusudra to build a boat."],["Atrahasis and Ea","The Akkadian version of the same story; Ea is the Akkadian name of Enki."],["Noah and Yahweh","In Genesis, God warns Noah to build the ark before a flood sent to punish human wickedness."]]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_deluge", artist:"Michelangelo", title:"The Deluge", date:"1508–09", det:"Fresco · Sistine Chapel ceiling, Vatican",
   fn:"One of the nine Genesis scenes on the ceiling of the pope’s chapel, commissioned by Julius II.",
   sum:"Michelangelo shows desperate families climbing to high ground while Noah’s ark floats in the distance. Placed among Greek myths, the scene shows how widely the flood story spread. Each culture gives it a different reason: overpopulation in Mesopotamia, human wickedness in Genesis and in the Greek version.",
   bul:["Genesis flood on the Sistine ceiling","Refugees in the foreground, ark behind","Same story, different reasons"], terms:[]},
  {type:"work", key:"h4_deucalion", artist:"Peter Paul Rubens", title:"Deucalion and Pyrrha", date:"1636–37", det:"Oil on panel (sketch) · Museo del Prado, Madrid",
   fn:"An oil sketch for a lost canvas by Jan Cossiers for the Torre de la Parada series; assistants used sketches like this to paint the full-size canvases.",
   sum:"In the Greek version, told most fully by Ovid, Zeus floods the world after the king Lycaon serves him human flesh. Only the good couple Deucalion, son of Prometheus, and Pyrrha, daughter of Epimetheus and Pandora, survive in a chest or boat. Told by an oracle to throw “the bones of their mother” behind them, they toss stones, the bones of Mother Earth, which become new men and women.",
   bul:["Zeus’s flood punishes cannibalism (Lycaon)","Survivors: Deucalion and Pyrrha","Stones become a new humankind"],
   terms:[["Deucalion and Pyrrha","The pious couple who survive the Greek flood and repopulate the earth by throwing stones."]]},
  {type:"theme", title:"A world in decline", img:"h4_deluge", stmt:"Greek myth tells human history as a fall, not as progress.",
   para:"Hesiod’s ages run downhill from gold to iron, and the flood wipes the slate almost clean. Humans fall from a god-like life because of Prometheus’s tricks and Pandora’s jar, much as Adam and Eve are expelled from Eden. Later, the Roman poet Virgil imagined the ages repeating in a cycle, so that Augustus could announce the return of a Golden Age.",
   bul:["History as decline: gold to iron","Flood as divine reset","Rome promised a new Golden Age"],
   terms:[], works:["h4_cranach","h4_flood","h4_deluge","h4_deucalion"],
   quote:{q:"For now truly is a race of iron, and men never rest from labour and sorrow by day, and from perishing by night", by:"Hesiod, Works and Days 176–178, trans. Evelyn-White"}}
  ]}
 ]
};
